--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Dependencies
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Plumbline depends on NumPy (BSD-3-Clause) at runtime.

numba (BSD-2-Clause) is an optional extra, used only on platforms with no
compiled wheel. It is not required and not installed by default.

No dependency of this project is under a copyleft licence. That is
deliberate: the software most in need of a correct lossless-JPEG decoder is
commercial medical imaging software and medical device firmware, and a
copyleft dependency would put this library out of their reach.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Not a medical device
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

This software decodes medical images. It is NOT a medical device, has not
been submitted to any regulator, and must not be relied upon as the basis of
a diagnosis. Anyone incorporating it into a regulated product is responsible
for their own verification and validation; see CORRECTNESS.md for what this
project does and does not claim, and for the evidence behind those claims.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Test data
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

No patient data is contained in this repository, and none may ever be added
to it. The synthetic conformance corpus is generated by the test suite. The
real-world discs used during development stay outside this repository and are
not distributed. A pre-commit hook in .githooks/ refuses files that look like
medical images; enable it with:

    git config core.hooksPath .githooks

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Test frames from the NCI Imaging Data Commons
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

`tests/data/idc/` holds eight lossless JPEG frames taken from public
research collections via the NCI Imaging Data Commons. They cover PET and
scanner firmware from outside the region the rest of the corpus came from.

They are bare JPEG frames — the entropy-coded image only. No DICOM tags,
and so nothing identifying a patient, travelled with them.

Each is redistributed under the Creative Commons Attribution licence its
collection carries, with the attribution that licence requires:

